### What is idempotency precisely?

The definition is about state, not about the response being byte-identical. An operation is idempotent if the resulting state after one call is the same as after many identical calls.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IdempotencyDefinition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Idempotent: SET balance = 1000000
        BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("1000000.00");
        balance = new BigDecimal("1000000.00"); // same state
        balance = new BigDecimal("1000000.00"); // still same state
        System.out.println("After 3 SET operations: " + balance);

        // NOT idempotent: balance = balance + 10000
        BigDecimal bal2 = new BigDecimal("1000000.00");
        bal2 = bal2.add(new BigDecimal("10000.00")); // 1010000
        bal2 = bal2.add(new BigDecimal("10000.00")); // 1020000
        bal2 = bal2.add(new BigDecimal("10000.00")); // 1030000
        System.out.println("After 3 ADD operations: " + bal2);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
After 3 SET operations: 1000000.00
After 3 ADD operations: 1030000.00
```

### Which HTTP methods are idempotent?

GET and HEAD only read. PUT replaces a resource. DELETE leaves it deleted. OPTIONS describes capabilities. POST creates or processes, so two identical POSTs usually do the thing twice.

| Method | Idempotent | Safe | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| GET | Yes | Yes | Only reads, no state change |
| HEAD | Yes | Yes | Only reads headers |
| PUT | Yes | No | Replaces resource with given value |
| DELETE | Yes | No | Deleting already-deleted is still deleted |
| OPTIONS | Yes | Yes | Describes capabilities |
| POST | No | No | Creates new resource or runs a command |
| PATCH | No | No | Relative update can change state each time |

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class HttpMethodIdempotency {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("PUT /accounts/SAV2001 {balance: 1000000}");
        System.out.println("  Call 1: sets balance to 1000000");
        System.out.println("  Call 2: sets balance to 1000000 (same state)");
        System.out.println("  -> Idempotent: state unchanged after repeat");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("DELETE /accounts/SAV2001");
        System.out.println("  Call 1: deletes account, returns 204");
        System.out.println("  Call 2: account already gone, returns 404");
        System.out.println("  -> Idempotent: state is 'deleted' either way");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("POST /transfers {from: SAV2001, to: SAV2002, amount: 10000}");
        System.out.println("  Call 1: transfers 10000");
        System.out.println("  Call 2: transfers 10000 AGAIN");
        System.out.println("  -> NOT idempotent: Priya gets 20000 instead of 10000");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
PUT /accounts/SAV2001 {balance: 1000000}
  Call 1: sets balance to 1000000
  Call 2: sets balance to 1000000 (same state)
  -> Idempotent: state unchanged after repeat

DELETE /accounts/SAV2001
  Call 1: deletes account, returns 204
  Call 2: account already gone, returns 404
  -> Idempotent: state is 'deleted' either way

POST /transfers {from: SAV2001, to: SAV2002, amount: 10000}
  Call 1: transfers 10000
  Call 2: transfers 10000 AGAIN
  -> NOT idempotent: Priya gets 20000 instead of 10000
```

### Why does idempotency matter in microservices?

The network is unreliable at every layer. Clients time out and retry. Load balancers retry idempotent methods automatically. Mobile apps on flaky connections resend.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class WhyItMatters {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Retry sources in a microservice system:");
        System.out.println("  1. Client timeout -> mobile app resends request");
        System.out.println("  2. Load balancer -> retries GET/PUT on connection failure");
        System.out.println("  3. API gateway -> retries on 503");
        System.out.println("  4. Kafka consumer -> at-least-once redelivery");
        System.out.println("  5. Saga orchestrator -> retries compensating transactions");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("If the operation is idempotent: all retries are harmless");
        System.out.println("If NOT: double charge, duplicate loan, double interest");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Retry sources in a microservice system:
  1. Client timeout -> mobile app resends request
  2. Load balancer -> retries GET/PUT on connection failure
  3. API gateway -> retries on 503
  4. Kafka consumer -> at-least-once redelivery
  5. Saga orchestrator -> retries compensating transactions

If the operation is idempotent: all retries are harmless
If NOT: double charge, duplicate loan, double interest
```

### How do you make POST idempotent with an idempotency key?

The client generates a unique key per logical operation. The server stores the key with the first result. A retry with the same key returns the stored result.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

@Service
public class IdempotentTransferService {

    private final Map<String, TransferResult> idempotencyStore = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public TransferResult transfer(String idempotencyKey, String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Check if this key was already processed
        TransferResult existing = idempotencyStore.get(idempotencyKey);
        if (existing != null) {
            System.out.println("Idempotency key " + idempotencyKey + " already processed, replaying");
            return existing;
        }

        // Process the transfer
        TransferResult result = new TransferResult("TXN-001", "COMPLETED", amount);
        idempotencyStore.put(idempotencyKey, result);
        System.out.println("Transfer processed: " + amount + " from " + from + " to " + to);
        return result;
    }

    record TransferResult(String txnId, String status, BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

```text
Expected output (first call):
Transfer processed: 50000.00 from SAV2001 to SAV2002

Expected output (retry with same key):
Idempotency key IK-20240115-001 already processed, replaying
```

### What is the common mistake with GET?

Hiding a non-idempotent side effect behind GET is a serious bug because intermediaries are allowed to retry GET freely.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class GetSideEffectBug {
    // WRONG: GET that computes and persists interest
    // GET /accounts/SAV2001/interest
    // -> calculates interest and SAVES it to the database
    // Load balancer retries on timeout -> interest posted TWICE

    // CORRECT: GET should only READ
    // POST /accounts/SAV2001/interest/compute
    // -> calculates and saves interest
    // GET /accounts/SAV2001/interest
    // -> reads the computed interest (safe to retry)

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: GET /interest that writes to DB");
        System.out.println("  Load balancer retries -> interest posted twice");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: POST to compute, GET to read");
        System.out.println("  POST is protected with idempotency key");
        System.out.println("  GET only reads -> safe to retry any number of times");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: GET /interest that writes to DB
  Load balancer retries -> interest posted twice

CORRECT: POST to compute, GET to read
  POST is protected with idempotency key
  GET only reads -> safe to retry any number of times
```

### How does PATCH differ from PUT?

PUT replaces the entire resource (idempotent). PATCH applies a partial update, which may or may not be idempotent depending on the operation.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class PutVsPatch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("PUT /accounts/SAV2001 {balance: 1000000}");
        System.out.println("  Always sets balance to 1000000 -> idempotent");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("PATCH /accounts/SAV2001 {operation: 'increment', amount: 100}");
        System.out.println("  Call 1: balance + 100 = 1000100");
        System.out.println("  Call 2: balance + 100 = 1000200");
        System.out.println("  -> NOT idempotent: different state on each call");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("PATCH can be idempotent if body is an absolute value:");
        System.out.println("  PATCH /accounts/SAV2001 {email: 'rajesh@nbi.com'}");
        System.out.println("  -> Sets email to same value each time -> idempotent");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
PUT /accounts/SAV2001 {balance: 1000000}
  Always sets balance to 1000000 -> idempotent

PATCH /accounts/SAV2001 {operation: 'increment', amount: 100}
  Call 1: balance + 100 = 1000100
  Call 2: balance + 100 = 1000200
  -> NOT idempotent: different state on each call

PATCH can be idempotent if body is an absolute value:
  PATCH /accounts/SAV2001 {email: 'rajesh@nbi.com'}
  -> Sets email to same value each time -> idempotent
```

### What about idempotency in async consumers?

At-least-once delivery in Kafka is the messaging equivalent of a client retry. Consumers must be idempotent too.

```java
package com.bank.idempotency;

public class AsyncIdempotency {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("HTTP retry: client sends same request twice");
        System.out.println("Kafka retry: broker delivers same message twice");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Same problem, same solution:");
        System.out.println("  1. Check if message_id already processed");
        System.out.println("  2. If yes: skip and acknowledge");
        System.out.println("  3. If no: process, save message_id, acknowledge");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
HTTP retry: client sends same request twice
Kafka retry: broker delivers same message twice

Same problem, same solution:
  1. Check if message_id already processed
  2. If yes: skip and acknowledge
  3. If no: process, save message_id, acknowledge
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Model true replacements as PUT, keep reads side-effect-free, protect non-idempotent commands with an idempotency key, and apply the same discipline to async consumers.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  - Model updates as PUT (replace) when possible -> naturally idempotent
  - Never hide writes behind GET -> load balancers retry GET freely
  - POST for commands that create or transfer -> add idempotency key
  - Idempotency key scope: per logical operation, not per HTTP attempt
  - Key store: Redis with TTL or DB with unique constraint
  - Handle concurrent same-key: atomic claim, wait or 409 for second
  - Async consumers: same idempotency check for at-least-once delivery
```
